        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.oe.3-5" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">315</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">nur Cb; fehlt HLbS</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">only Cb; lacking in HLbS</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.oe.4-4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">315</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">A. (<hi rend="italic">verb. aus</hi> Angliam) t. <hi rend="italic">Cb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">A. (corrected from Angliam) t. Cb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">203</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Nom.-<hi rend="italic">e</hi> in B's <hi rend="italic">bote</hi> ist Mittelenglisch</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The nom. -<hi rend="italic">e</hi> in B’s <hi rend="italic">bote</hi> is Middle English</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">203</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">census</hi> (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Wb</hi>) setzt für ‘Geld’ Cons auch sonst, aber auch Q und Domesday I 179a 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Cons uses <hi rend="italic">census</hi> (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Wb</hi>) for ‘money’ elsewhere too, but so do Q and Domesday I 179a 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">203</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Von <hi rend="italic">bondan</hi> missversteht Inst Numerus und Bed.; <hi rend="italic">s. o</hi>. VI Atr. 32². Der Bauer war bereits um 1100 herabgedrückt und um 1150 hörig</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Inst misunderstands the number and meaning of <hi rend="italic">bondan</hi>; <hi rend="italic">see above</hi> VI Atr. 32². The peasant had already been depressed in status by c. 1100, and by c. 1150 was unfree</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_8.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">203</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Plural, wie Q versteht, kann <hi rend="italic">þeode</hi> wohl um 1114, aber nicht unter Æthelred sein; streiche <hi rend="italic">Wb</hi> S. 221 Sp. 3 Z. 29</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">þeode</hi> can probably be plural, as Q understands it, around 1114, but not under Æthelred; delete <hi rend="italic">Wb</hi> p. 221 col. 3 l. 29</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_8.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">352</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. VI Atr. 31–32, 1.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_8.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">352</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Both are actually singular. Inst. translates <hi rend="italic">bondan</hi> by <hi rend="italic">pauperibus hominibus</hi>, cf. VII Atr. 2, 4 and note. In reality, however, the Norse word <hi rend="italic">bondi</hi> denotes any free members of the community, whether rich or poor, other than persons in official positions.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_8.robnote.fn.3" n="3" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">178</biblScope></bibl><label>fal[s]e</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">falfe</hi> B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="IICn_8.whinote.n.5" n="5" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">463</biblScope></bibl><label>8</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">This section is taken from VI Ethelred, 32, 32.1 (cf. V. 26.1), the latter clause being itself from III Edgar, 8.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="IICn_8.whinote.n.6" n="6" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">463</biblScope></bibl><label>householder</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">bonda. see</hi> p. 447, n. 6. In this context the word refers to any law-abiding member of society. As in the laws of the Scandinavians, the free peasant proprietor is taken as the normal person to benefit from the law.</seg></note>
